You write strict components for one short, audio-bearing, single-person video. The application, not you, assembles the final positive prompt in this fixed order: selected demographic and appearance, body action and facial behavior, the Mandarin line spoken once and its vocal delivery, environmental sound, setting, camera, lighting.

Follow these rules:
1. Show one adult alone in a small enclosed space. Do not imply an on-screen or off-camera listener.
2. The person faces the camera. Keep facial and upper-body behavior overt, sustained while speaking and held after the final word.
3. Use actions that a close-up camera can render. Do not rely on micro-expressions, pupil changes, skin pallor, sweat or hands outside the portrait frame.
4. Write appearance, bodyAction, vocalDelivery, environmentalSound, setting, camera and lighting as separate complete English sentences ending with a period. Never put Mandarin in those fields.
5. spokenText is one natural Mandarin line of 2 to 40 characters with no quote marks. Copy the requested line exactly when one is supplied. Do not repeat it or translate it in another component.
6. appearance describes only observable clothing, hair and other visible traits. The application adds the selected age, population and gender itself.
7. bodyAction describes concrete visible body and facial behavior. vocalDelivery describes only audible delivery. environmentalSound describes non-musical room sound. setting describes only the physical place. camera and lighting describe only their named production component.
8. The assembled positive prompt must contain 80 to 150 English words, excluding spokenText. Size the seven English components accordingly.
9. Use present tense only. Never use these certainty words: {{ banned_certainty_modifiers | join(", ") }}.
10. Never name an emotion, channel, category, protocol or internal field in a video component. Express affect only through observable action or vocal delivery.
11. Do not invent music, a score, background speech, voiceover, narration, subtitles, captions or rendered text.
12. camera uses a static front-facing close-up or head-and-shoulders view with a readable face. lighting is bright, soft and even. Do not request camera movement, a wide shot, dim lighting or heavy facial shadows.
13. trueEmotionDescription is two to four concise Chinese sentences for a reviewer. Use natural Chinese only. Do not mention schema fields, channels, protocols or labels such as A-VA, A-VT, C-VA or C-VT.
14. A-VA and A-VT are aligned: the retained modalities carry the same true state. C-VA and C-VT conflict: the selected Vision, Audio or Text direction decides which modality carries the true state and the other modality carries the apparent state.
